      "content": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <climits>\n#include <omp.h>\n\nint main() {\n\tint n, s;\n\tstd::cin >> n >> s;\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<int>> matrix(n, std::vector<int>(n));\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j)\n\t\t\tscanf(\"%d\", &matrix[i][j]);\n\n\t// Integral image: P of size (n+1) x (n+1), initialized to zero.\n\tstd::vector<std::vector<long long>> P(n + 1, std::vector<long long>(n + 1, 0));\n\n\t// Step 1: row-wise prefix sums – parallel over rows with static scheduling.\n\t#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long row_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\trow_sum += matrix[i][j];\n\t\t\tP[i + 1][j + 1] = row_sum;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// Step 2: column-wise accumulation – parallel over columns with static scheduling.\n\t#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\tlong long col_cum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\t\tcol_cum += P[i + 1][j + 1];\n\t\t\tP[i + 1][j + 1] = col_cum;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// Search all top-left corners using collapsed loops, reduction and static scheduling.\n\tlong long mxSum = LLONG_MIN;\n\t#pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) schedule(static) reduction(max:mxSum)\n\tfor (int r = 0; r <= n - s; ++r) {\n\t\tfor (int c = 0; c <= n - s; ++c) {\n\t\t\tlong long sum = P[r + s][c + s] - P[r][c + s] - P[r + s][c] + P[r][c];\n\t\t\tif (sum > mxSum)\n\t\t\t\tmxSum = sum;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tprintf(\"%d\\n\", static_cast<int>(mxSum));\n\treturn 0;\n}\n",
      "path": "src/solution.cpp"
    }
  ],
  "hypothesis": "Replace the sliding-window algorithm with an integral image (prefix-sum) approach. Compute row-wise cumulative sums in parallel over rows (#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)), then complete the integral image via column-wise accumulation in parallel over columns (#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)). Finally, search all top-left corners using collapsed loops with reduction(max:mxSum) and static scheduling to evenly distribute work. This demonstrates OpenMP work sharing (parallel for on both construction and search phases), reductions (max reduction), explicit static scheduling for balanced workload, and thread granularity via collapse(2).",
